<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saad Zaghloul</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1859–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saad Zaghloul was an Egyptian nationalist leader and founder of the Wafd party. His arrest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> by the British in March 1919, after he sought to put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s case for independence at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>, set off the Egyptian Revolution of 1919. He led the Wafd to victory in the 1924 elections and served briefly as prime minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saad-zaghloul.jpg" alt="Saad Zaghloul" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. Hanselman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ModernEgypt,_Saad_Zaghloul,_BAP_14785.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Saad Zaghloul was born in the village of Ibyanah in the Kafr el-Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>. He attended Al-Azhar University and a French law school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. As a lawyer he gained wealth and status, and his familiarity with the Egyptian countryside and its idioms helped his success. His marriage to the daughter of the prime minister Mustafa Fahmi Pasha, whose friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evelyn-baring-1st-earl-of-cromer">Lord Cromer</a> helped make Zaghloul acceptable to the British occupation, took him into the upper class.</p><p>He served as a judge, as minister of education from 1906 to 1908 and as minister of justice from 1910 to 1912, and in 1913 became vice-president of the Legislative Assembly. His reforms were acceptable to both nationalists and the British, and he kept clear of extreme factions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1882 and declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> at the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In 1919 Zaghloul led a delegation, a wafd, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> to demand recognition of the independence and unity of Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. When he refused to end his agitation, the British exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, and moved him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> in 1922 because of ill health. He was released in 1923. His absence caused disturbances that led to the Egyptian Revolution of 1919. In the Seychelles he met other exiled anti-imperialist leaders and, like them, got letters past the colonial censorship.</p><p>On his return he led the nationalist forces, and the elections of 12 January 1924 gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wafd-party">Wafd Party</a> an overwhelming majority. Two weeks later he formed the first Wafdist government and served as prime minister from 26 January to 24 November 1924. The historian P. J. Vatikiotis wrote that the masses saw him as the uncompromising national hero, although he had come to power partly by compromising with the palace group and accepting the conditions that safeguarded British interests. After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-stack">Sir Lee Stack</a>, Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, on 19 November 1924, and British demands that Zaghloul found unacceptable, he resigned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zaghloul returned to active politics two years later but never again held the premiership, though he remained extremely influential until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 23 August 1927. He was buried near the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">al-Shafi'i</a>, and his remains were later moved to a mausoleum built for him in 1936, Beit El-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>, where his wife Safiya Khānūm, a feminist and revolutionary, is also buried.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saad Zaghloul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The exile of <b>Saad Zaghloul</b> in March 1919 set off the first modern revolution, and on 22 February 1922 Britain declared Egypt independent as a kingdom under Fuad I. Defeat in the First Arab-Israeli War of 1948 turned feeling against the monarchy; in 1951 Egypt renounced the 1936…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1919 the British exile of <b>Saad Zaghloul</b> and the Wafd delegation sparked a nationwide revolution.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-egypt">Sultanate of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Metoubes, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.29,30.52&amp;year=1903" aria-label="Metoubes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5338 1024 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saad Zaghloul"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 1294h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 1294h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.29,30.52&amp;year=1903">What was here in 1903? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
